From 004f079985106ae892cc0be9de07ba966556357d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 01:41:32 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Graceful restores half your run energy again, and it is six
 numbers

Backlog item 5: "Graceful gives +50% energy recovery". It paid +20% - OSRS's own per-piece
3/4/4/3/3/3 - and OSRS pays 30% at most, so this is a large departure asked for by name. Two
things were settled before anything was built: 50 is the new TOTAL rather than 50 on top of the
20, and the whole 50 comes from the pieces themselves.

NO ENGINE CHANGE, AND NO SCRIPT. calculateRunWeight() already sums the energy_restore param over
worn equipment into runrestore, and updateEnergy() already scales natural recovery by it:

    const recovered = natural + (((natural * this.runrestore) / 100) | 0);

So the whole feature is six param= lines. That is what the earlier round bought by putting the
mechanism in the numbers the engine reads anyway instead of in a ticking script, and this is the
first time that choice has been cashed in.

PER PIECE, NOT A SET BONUS. The alternative was OSRS's per-piece numbers plus a large bonus for
wearing all six, which was offered and not chosen. It would have cost the set mechanism the engine
deliberately does not have - it sums a param and knows nothing about which outfit a piece belongs
to - and it would have made four pieces worth little. OSRS's own +10% for the full set is still
not implemented and now does not need to be: 50 already clears OSRS's 30 by twenty points, so a
set bonus on top would be a departure on a departure.

THE SIX NUMBERS ARE OSRS'S SHAPE x 2.5, so a 4% piece stays worth more than a 3% piece in the same
proportion: 4 -> 10, and 3 -> 7.5, which is not a whole percent. The two 4% pieces land on 10
exactly. The four 3% pieces have to sum to 30, so two take 8 and two take 7.

WHICH two is decided by the piece's own weight reduction and not by taste. Boots and cape take 4kg
off and take 8; hood and gloves take 3kg off and take 7. That is OSRS's own principle at a finer
grain - its 4% pieces are its two heaviest reductions, -6 and -5, and its 3% pieces are the -4s
and the -3s - and it turns the choice into a property a check can hold: recovery is non-decreasing
in weight reduction, and equal reductions recover equally. legs 10, top 10, boots 8, cape 8,
hood 7, gloves 7.

    at Agility 1  the natural 8 a tick becomes 12
    at Agility 99 the natural 24 a tick becomes 36

tools/gracefulspec.json holds the wiki figures, the chosen total, the scaling and the tie-break.
The wiki was re-read for this round rather than taken from the earlier round's notes, because this
round rewrites the comment that states what OSRS pays.

THE EXPECTED NUMBERS ARE NOW IN ONE PLACE. tools/objpacked.py had them typed into a GRACE dict of
its own; it reads the spec instead. So the artefact check asks whether the packed obj table agrees
with the spec, group 64b asks whether the configs do, and a spec edited alone goes red both ways
instead of quietly redefining what correct means.

SIX STALE COMMENTS, and the same fault this project keeps finding. Every Graceful record said "its
own effect is in outfit_effects.rs2", which is the one file that says in capitals that Graceful is
NOT in it. Each now names the two lines in its own record that are the effect, what OSRS pays for
that piece, and where the departure is written down. The nine records that really do have their
effect in that file are untouched.

CHECKED WITHOUT A BUILD, WHICH IS NEW. Group 64b's existing Graceful checks read the packed
artefact and the engine source, and the note in poh_mutate.py says no mutation to this tree can
reach either. Seventeen checks were added that read the configs and the spec: the spec adds up to
the total it claims, the configs agree with it piece by piece, recovery is monotonic in weight
reduction, ties match, the weights are still OSRS's, no other obj carries the param, no script
reads it, and the departure is stated in both places a reader would look. Each sweep carries a
guard that fails if it read nothing, because "found no other carriers" and "read no files" print
the same green line otherwise.

AND A MODEL, so the in-game check is a pass or a fail rather than a feeling. The first Graceful
round's doc said to "confirm the recovery differs by about a fifth from bare", which is not
something anybody can check. tools/gracefulsim.py prints the tick counts: resting from empty to
full takes 1250 ticks at Agility 1 bare and 834 in the set, 417 and 278 at 99 - exactly 1.5x at
every level the rounding lands clean. It quotes all four lines of updateEnergy()'s arithmetic and
refuses to print anything if one of them has moved, and the battery runs it, so a green line there
is a statement about the engine's source today rather than about a copy of it taken once.

It also prints the thing most likely to be got wrong by hand: five of the six pieces measure
between 40% and 43% depending on which is missing, never 50%, because there is no set bonus.

18 new mutations, each anchored on a pattern confirmed to appear exactly once in its file - this
harness replaces the first match and has no uniqueness rule of its own. The two guard checks are
mutated in the checker itself, which is the only place a claim about a sweep can be broken.

The sandbox builds again, so the artefact check ran here for the first time: 8,385 packed objs,
all named, negative weights intact, 50 read back out of data/pack/server/obj.dat.

AND A CRASH THAT WAS BEING REPORTED AS A DISAGREEMENT, found by test-applying this patch to a
fresh clone. objpacked.py reads pack/param.pack, which is GENERATED - the deploy deletes it and
the build writes it again - so a clone with no build has obj.pack and not param.pack, and the tool
raised FileNotFoundError from inside main(). The battery turned that into a FAIL whose explanation
was empty, which reads exactly like the packed numbers disagreeing. It now returns 2 with a
sentence saying which pack file is missing and that the build writes it, and the battery prints
that sentence. Eleventh time in this project that a check's own condition raising has been
mistaken for the check firing.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
